<article class="env-lemma" id="08XC">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/08XC">Lemma <span data-tag="08XC">35.4.24</span>.</a> If $M \in \text{Mod}_ R$ is flat, then $C(M)$ is an injective $R$-module. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $0 \to N \to P \to Q \to 0$ be an exact sequence in $\text{Mod}_ R$. Since $M$ is flat, 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  0 \to N \otimes _ R M \to P \otimes _ R M \to Q \otimes _ R M \to 0  \]
</div>
    <p>
       is exact. By Lemma <a href="/tag/08WR" data-tag="08WR">35.4.10</a>, 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  0 \to C(Q \otimes _ R M) \to C(P \otimes _ R M) \to C(N \otimes _ R M) \to 0  \]
</div>
    <p>
       is exact. By (<a href="/tag/08WT" data-tag="08WT">35.4.11.1</a>), this last sequence can be rewritten as 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  0 \to \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _ R(Q, C(M)) \to \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _ R(P, C(M)) \to \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _ R(N, C(M)) \to 0.  \]
</div>
    <p>
       Hence $C(M)$ is an injective object of $\text{Mod}_ R$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>